# {{ .name }}

.NET streaming extractor integration for files too large to ride the message
broker. One run = one sweep: the runner lists the files under the incoming
prefix, streams each record-by-record in constant memory through its
registered streamed extractor, writes the transformed output to storage,
publishes one claim-check CloudEvent per file (a reference to the output,
never the payload) to the `{{ .topic }}` topic on the Dapr pub/sub, archives
the consumed input, and exits. Scheduling lives outside the block —
activation cadence is deployment configuration (a Kubernetes CronJob in
production); locally the system host runs the block once at startup.

## How a run flows

```
scheduled run (CronJob / host)
   │
   ▼
list files under incoming/
   │  empty ──▶ quiet exit 0
   ▼
per file:
   │
   ▼
stream records ──▶ validate + transform ──▶ JSONL output (output/)
   │
   ▼
publish one claim-check CloudEvent to '{{ .topic }}'
   │  (reference to the output; subject = source file name)
   ▼
archive the consumed input (incoming/ → processed/)
   │
   ▼
exit 0
```

Off the happy path: a file whose entity type has no registered extractor
routes a business incident (and stays in place), a zero-valid-record file is
archived without an event, and a technical failure (dead source, dead broker)
leaves the file for the next run and fails the run with exit 1 — the CronJob
records it. Sweep idempotency is the archive's contract: a processed file is
invisible to the next sweep, so a completed file never re-runs.

`EventSource`/`EventType` in `src/Constants.cs` come from the scaffold
parameters `eventSource`/`eventType`; unset, they derive as
`urn:<organization>:<app-id>` and
`<organization-lower>.<first-topic-segment>.<last-topic-segment>`. Set them
when migrating an existing component so consumers keep seeing the same
envelope.

## The claim-check contract

The published event carries a **reference**, not the payload: the output's
location and file name, the source file name, the entity type, and the
record/reject counts. The consuming component subscribes to `{{ .topic }}`,
deserializes the reference, and streams the named file from the same storage
behind the source port. The file-adapter kind and the prefix layout are part
of the system contract, on equal footing with the topic and the contract
record — the consumer must resolve the reference through the same native
storage SDK, never a Dapr binding (bindings buffer the payload through the
sidecar, defeating constant-memory streaming of large files).

## Configuration

Settings live in `src/appsettings.json`, overridable per environment via
`appsettings.{Environment}.json` and environment variables; deployment
overlays the file via ConfigMap. `IConfiguration` is in DI. File layout
(incoming/output/processed prefixes) binds to
`src/Configuration/IngestOptions.cs` from the `Ingest` section.

## Build and test

The framework packages are a preview build (`0.3.0-local.1`) until the next
published beta. Restore resolves them from the developer-machine
`intropy-local` feed — a folder source registered in the user-level
`~/.nuget/NuGet/NuGet.Config`, not in this repo, so each machine points it at
its own local framework build output. Without that source, restore fails with
NU1101 (package not found) — add the feed or wait for the published beta.

```bash
task build
task test
```

The extractor does not run standalone. It runs via its system host, which
runs it once at startup, provides the source binding, pub/sub, and platform
services, and carries the sample data used to seed a run. A re-run is a host
restart. `task test` covers the component-level loop: sweep-run integration
tests plus the composition smoke test, all without a sidecar
(`task test:integration`). `task coverage` runs the integration suite with
branch coverage (Cobertura report under the integration project's
`TestResults/`).

## Testing strategy

The integration suite runs against `Intropy.Framework.Testing` fakes — no
Dapr sidecar, no Testcontainers, no live platform services. The one
principle throughout: **resolve the sweep exactly as production composition
registers it, fake only the edges, assert on fake state.**

### Layers

| Layer | Project / files | What it pins |
|---|---|---|
| Sweep run | `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/SweepRunTests.cs` | The full sweep (list → per-file streamed extraction → publish reference → archive) over the incoming prefix, with `FakeTopic` at the publish edge and an in-memory source adapter. Pins the consumption contract: a delivered file streams, publishes one claim-check reference, and is archived; an empty prefix is a quiet clean run; a publish failure leaves the file in place. |
| Composition | `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/CompositionTests.cs` | The real DI graph from `Composition` builds without a sidecar and the keyed source adapter resolves. |

### The edges and their fakes

- **Source files** — `InMemoryFileAdapter` (implements the streaming
  `IFileStreamAdapter` contract: commit-on-dispose writes, read-after-N-bytes
  fault injection). Seeded with delivered files; asserts read file state
  directly (`Files`).
- **Topic** — `FakeTopic<TCtx>`, swapped into DI in place of the production
  `SendStep<TCtx>` registration (a `DaprTopicPublisher`), exactly like the
  other externals. `Events` exposes exactly what the real publisher would
  have encoded; `SendException` simulates a dead broker.
- **Platform services** — `FakeIdempotencyServiceClient` and
  `FakeBusinessIncidentServiceClient`, swapped the same way.

### Behaviors pinned

- **One reference event per file.** Record/reject counts come from the
  streamed context; the event subject is the source file name.
- **Archive is the idempotency mechanism.** The consumed input moves to the
  processed prefix only after a successful publish; the next sweep never
  re-reads an archived file.
- **Empty prefix is clean.** A run with nothing under incoming/ completes
  quietly (exit 0) without publishing.

